    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nUsing `min()`/`max()` on `2 ** int(exponent)` gives the wrong result when the exponent is negative. \nComparing two of these powers with `==` also segfaults.\n\nBoth examples below work with 3.2.9 and fail with 3.3.0 and `master`.\n\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n```python\ndef clamp(exponent, maximum=16):\n    return min(max(2 ** int(exponent), 1), maximum)\n\n\ndef equal_powers(left, right):\n    return 2 ** int(left) == 2 ** int(right)\n```\n\n```console\n$ pixi run python -m Cython.Build.Cythonize -i reproducer.py\n$ pixi run python -c 'import reproducer; print(reproducer.clamp(-4))'\n16\n```\n\nThe comparison segfaults:\n\n```bash\npixi run python -c 'import reproducer; print(reproducer.equal_powers(-4, -4))'\n```\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nIn Python, `clamp(-4)` returns `1` and `equal_powers(-4, -4)` returns `True`.\n\n### OS\n\nLinux x86_64\n\n### Python version\n\n3.12.14\n\n### Cython version\n\n3.3.0\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_",
